# Activate the environment. Use source .venv/bin/activate on Bash.
.\.venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1

# Install Flask, NumPy, redis-py, Microsoft Entra authentication, and dependencies.
# pip install -r requirements.txt

# Run the Flask application.
python app.py

# Open http://localhost:5000 in a browser.

# Vector search concepts:
# An embedding represents an item as a list of numbers. Similar items have
# vectors that are close together according to a distance metric such as cosine.
# Redis stores the vector bytes in a hash and RediSearch finds nearby vectors.

# Load sample data and perform a similarity search.
# 1. Select Load Sample Products in Data Operations.
# 2. Select List All Products and confirm product keys appear in the results.
# 3. Enter product:001 in Similarity Search, set top_k to 5, and select Find Similar.
# 4. Review the matching products and their distance scores.

# Store a new product and search again.
# Select Store Product with these values:
# Product key: product:011
# Embedding: [0.53, 0.63, 0.58, 0.37, 0.68, 0.47, 0.73, 0.57]
# Metadata:
# product_id=011
# name=Gym Bag
# category=Sports

# Search for product:009 and review how the similarity ordering changes after
# the new vector is added.

# Remove a product.
# Enter product:011 in Remove Product and select Remove.
# Select List All Products and confirm product:011 is no longer listed.

# What the application does behind the scenes:
# - Creates a RediSearch index with text fields and an embedding vector field.
# - Uses HNSW, an approximate nearest-neighbor algorithm, for fast search.
# - Stores vectors as FLOAT32 values with eight dimensions.
# - Uses cosine distance to compare the query vector with product vectors.
# - Uses the product: key prefix so the index includes product hashes.

# Stop the Flask app with Ctrl+C before continuing.

# Deactivate the virtual environment when finished.
deactivate

